### Nervous System - Summary

**Tumors:** Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant posterior fossa tumor in children, with small round blue cells and Homer-Wright rosettes. Schwannoma shows Antoni A (cellular with Verocay bodies) and Antoni B (loose, hypocellular) areas. Meningioma features whorls and psammoma bodies. Glioblastoma (WHO Grade 4) shows pseudopalisading necrosis and microvascular proliferation. Diffuse astrocytoma (Grade 2) has increased cellularity without mitoses or necrosis. Pilocytic astrocytoma (Grade 1) features Rosenthal fibers and eosinophilic granular bodies. Oligodendroglioma shows "fried egg" appearance with calcifications and 1p/19q codeletion. Ependymoma has perivascular pseudorosettes and true ependymal rosettes.

**Vascular Lesions:** Ischemic stroke shows red neurons (eosinophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic nuclei) in early phase and reactive gliosis in late phase. Subarachnoid hemorrhage is most commonly due to saccular aneurysm rupture.

**Infections:** Brain abscess shows central liquefactive necrosis with surrounding granulation tissue. Viral encephalitis features perivascular lymphocytic cuffing and microglial nodules. Prion diseases show spongiform vacuolization without inflammation.

**Degenerative Diseases:** Alzheimer disease shows senile plaques (A-beta) and neurofibrillary tangles (tau). Parkinson disease features Lewy bodies (alpha-synuclein positive) with substantia nigra pigment loss. Multiple sclerosis shows demyelination plaques with preserved axons.

**Peripheral Nerve:** Guillain-Barre syndrome shows segmental demyelination with onion bulb formations. Diabetic neuropathy features axonal degeneration with PAS-positive thickening of endoneurial vessel walls.
